\subsection{Noise-Free Model-Mismatch Floor}
\label{sec:model-mismatch-ablation}

A separate ablation removes simulated sensor corruption while leaving each
estimator otherwise in its deployed configuration.  The eight stationary
JONSWAP and PM--Stokes records are replayed through OU--II, OU--III, and TFG
with the simulator's \texttt{--no-noise} option.  Accelerometer and gyroscope
white noise, initial bias, bias random walk, and magnetometer noise and
calibration perturbations are therefore absent, while the physical wave and
attitude motion are unchanged.  The estimators are \emph{not} retuned for ideal
measurements: their process/measurement covariances, pseudo-measurements,
adaptation, startup logic, and regularization remain those used in normal
operation.  The experiment consequently measures a deployed
estimator/model-mismatch floor, not pure plant-model mismatch.

All values in Table~\ref{tab:model-mismatch-floor} pool the final \SI{900}{s}
of the eight equal-length records.  With identical window lengths, the pooled
RMS is the RMS obtained by concatenating the eight scored windows.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Noise-free deployed-estimator mismatch floor over eight stationary
  records.  $Z/Z_{\rm ref}$ is normalized by pooled reference vertical RMS.}
  \label{tab:model-mismatch-floor}
  \footnotesize
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.4pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
    \toprule
    Family & $X$ [m] & $Y$ [m] & $Z$ [m] & 3-D [m] & $Z/Z_{\rm ref}$ [\%] \\
    \midrule
    OU--II  & 0.358 & 0.248 & 0.237 & 0.496 & 20.09 \\
    OU--III & 0.277 & 0.200 & 0.139 & 0.369 & 11.79 \\
    TFG     & 0.539 & 0.204 & 0.149 & 0.595 & 12.65 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

OU--III has the lowest pooled displacement floor: vertical RMS is
\SI{0.139}{m} and 3-D RMS is \SI{0.369}{m}, compared with
\SI{0.237}{m} and \SI{0.496}{m} for OU--II.  TFG is close to OU--III
vertically at \SI{0.149}{m}, but its \SI{0.539}{m} $X$-axis floor drives the
3-D value to \SI{0.595}{m}.  The residual therefore does not vanish when the
sensor corruption is removed.  Figure~\ref{fig:model-mismatch-floor} shows the
same pooled comparison across all four displacement channels together with the
attitude and bias-state channels.  The three families share essentially one
roll floor (\SIrange{0.115}{0.118}{\degree}), so the attitude separation is
carried by pitch and yaw, where OU--III reaches \SI{0.073}{\degree} and
\SI{0.261}{\degree} against \SI{0.228}{\degree} and \SI{0.544}{\degree} for
OU--II and \SI{0.184}{\degree} and \SI{0.481}{\degree} for TFG.

\IfFileExists{ou_model_mismatch_floor.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_model_mismatch_floor}
    \caption{Pooled noise-free floor by channel over the eight stationary
    records.  Displacement and attitude panels use linear axes; the
    estimator-generated gyro-bias panel is logarithmic because TFG exceeds the
    two OU families by more than an order of magnitude.  The injected true
    sensor biases are identically zero, so the two lower panels report bias
    state created by the estimator rather than sensor-bias error.}
    \label{fig:model-mismatch-floor}
  \end{figure}
}{}

The per-record behavior in Fig.~\ref{fig:model-mismatch-scaling} identifies
what kind of floor this is.  Over a \num{31}:1 range of $H_s$ the residual
tracks the slope-one reference rather than flattening toward a fixed offset,
and the normalized panel is close to constant within each family: vertical
residual error stays at \pct{2.83}--\pct{3.41} of $H_s$ for OU--III,
\pct{4.91}--\pct{5.44} for OU--II, and \pct{3.02}--\pct{3.62} for TFG.  A
sensor-noise-limited floor would instead be roughly fixed in absolute terms and
would therefore fall as a fraction of $H_s$ as the sea grows.  The observed
amplitude-proportional behavior is consistent with a deterministic
estimator/regularization component that scales with the motion being tracked.
The third panel isolates where the families separate: the along-$x$ residual
carries the same proportionality but places TFG above both OU families at every
height, which is what drives its pooled 3-D floor.

\IfFileExists{ou_model_mismatch_scaling.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_model_mismatch_scaling}
    \caption{Per-record noise-free residual against significant wave height.
    Solid lines with filled markers are JONSWAP records; dashed lines with open
    markers are PM--Stokes.  The dotted guide in the two logarithmic panels has
    slope one.  The center panel normalizes the vertical residual by $H_s$ and
    is nearly flat, indicating an amplitude-proportional rather than a fixed
    error floor.}
    \label{fig:model-mismatch-scaling}
  \end{figure}
}{}

The zero-bias input also exposes estimator-generated bias states.  Pooled
accelerometer-bias-state RMS is \SI{0.0211}{m.s^{-2}} for OU--III,
\SI{0.0425}{m.s^{-2}} for OU--II, and \SI{0.0313}{m.s^{-2}} for TFG, and the
pooled gyro-bias-state RMS is \SI{9.70e-6}{rad.s^{-1}}, \SI{1.12e-5}{rad.s^{-1}},
and \SI{2.69e-4}{rad.s^{-1}} respectively.  These are not physical sensor-bias
errors in this experiment because the injected true bias is zero; they quantify
bias state used by each estimator while fitting ideal but model-mismatched
motion.

The result should not be attributed to the OU or TFG stochastic model alone.
The virtual measurements intentionally distort low-frequency physical motion,
the adaptive operating point has finite bandwidth, attitude error can feed
gravity into horizontal acceleration, and the filters retain measurement
covariances calibrated for real sensors.  The ablation therefore bounds the
combined residual of these deployed estimator choices under ideal input
measurements rather than isolating a single source of model error.
